n the process of agreeing mechanisms whereby it can continue work on the matter in substantive terms. We do not anticipate material financial impact on the conduct of the proceedings.&nbsp;&nbsp;Responses to Ignite’s queries: &nbsp;Key Personnel/Lead Lawyers - per the verbal request can the lead lawyers per NOx manufacturer and for the BHP matter be detailed to us please?&nbsp; The board of directors now comprises Alicia Alinia, Howard Morris, and Joseph Moreno of both the Parent and the Firm (the “Board”). Thomas Goodhead has now left both as CEO and Director of the Firm.&nbsp; The Board is responsible for the overall management of the Firm, and each member of the Board has statutory duties to exercise independent judgment in accordance with the Companies Act 2006. The Board appointments demonstrate that Pogust Goodhead is being run by an extremely experienced and committed team of independent, regulated, legal professionals. Each of the Board members is regulated by the SRA and qualified to practice in England &amp; Wales.&nbsp;Following Pete’s departure, Anna Varga, whom you have met, has taken the lead on Mercedes and Vauxhall cases and is overall supervisor for the remaining NOx cases. Working closely with our case leads, Anna will ensure a smooth transition and maintain consistency in how the cases are managed. For BHP, our case leads are Guy Robson and Faranak Ghajavand. Additionally, Craig Shuttleworth (Kastle Solicitors and previously Head of Litigation at Jones Day), who has decades of litigation experience is joining us as a strategic advisor. Craig has been working with the firm supporting BHP and other matters since 2023 and will work to support teams across both the NOx and BHP matters. We want to reassure you that the cases continue to receive the level of expertise and attention required. Our retained barristers and partner firms remain fully engaged and unchanged, providing continuity and stability in external representation. Internally, the team continues to operate with the senior oversight and leadership necessary to progress the cases effectively. We are confident that this structure offers the strongest platform for achieving a successful outcome.&nbsp;We would like to inform you that Michael Burke has formally tendered his resignation with Pogust Goodhead and is currently working his notice period. We will liaise with the Defendants in those Litigations to advise them accordingly. We will provide a more detailed response outlining further upcoming changes within the case teams, ensuring you have a clear understanding of the updated structure.&nbsp;Negotiating Partners - should they differ from 1, can the lawyers in charge of WP negotiations also be detailed to us please?These individuals are consistent with those mentioned above.&nbsp;Recent Costs Judgment - Last week a costs Judgment was published which saw the claimants budget reduced significantly for the trial. Does this cause any issues for PG and/or Gramercy? link: https://caselaw.nationalarchives.gov.uk/ewhc/kb/2025/2307The reductions that the court applied to the Quantum Trial budgets related mainly to costs submitted by non-lead claimant firms. The reductions applied to PG related costs were a relatively modest 30% after adjusting for non-lead claimant firm costs. This 30% reduction is modest both in the context of expected recoverable costs on success, and in the context of budget to adequately resource the case. Therefore, PG does not consider there to be any material effect on PG or Gramercy. Gramercy have also not raised any material concern.&nbsp;&nbsp;Happy to arrange a call to discuss, if helpful. &nbsp;Kind regards,Helena &nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Helena&nbsp;YearwoodRisk&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Compliance&nbsp;DirectorThis email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this emai