ent and so they would be happy to amend in relation to this point. We have therefore proposed this amendment. Can you confirm that you are happy for us to get this agreed with insurers?&nbsp;1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; In relation to any Dispute, on each occasion that the Law Firm receives Law Firm Success Fees, it shall within five business days of receipt pay the Insurers, up to the Insurer Proceeds, an amount equal to 50% of the aggregate amounts which have been paid by the Insurers in relation to Claims made under any of the ATE Insurance Policies less any Claims that: i.) have been repaid pursuant to clause 3 of the Priorities Deeds that relate to each of the ATE Insurance Policies or ii.) sums already paid under clause 2 or 3 of this Agreement.2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Where the Law Firm receives Law Firm Success Fees and no Claims have been paid by Insurers, or the Claims paid by Insurers do not exhaust the Insurer Proceeds, and the Insurers subsequently pay any Claim, then within five business days of the Insurer’s payment of the Claim, the Law Firm shall pay the Insurers, up to the Insurer Proceeds, or remaining Insurer Proceeds, as the case may be, an amount equal to 50% of the aggregate amounts paid by the Insurers less any Claims that: i.) have been repaid pursuant to clause 3 of the Priorities Deeds that relate to each of the ATE Insurance Policies or ii.) sums already paid under clause 2 or 3 of this Agreement.3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; The Law Firm shall make any payments required under this Clause 2 into the Insurer Distribution Account for distribution between the Insurers on a pro rata and pari passu basis in accordance with the total amount paid by the Insurers as at the Assessment Date. Payment to the Insurers from the Insurer Distribution Account shall be made in accordance with Clause 5. The obligations of the Law Firm under this Clause 2 are referred to herein as the “Obligation”.&nbsp;&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)FINEX Global&nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116)On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.comwww.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW.Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At WTW we work flexibly - whilst you may receive emails from me out of working hours, I do not expect a response or action outside your own working hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;From:&nbsp;James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;06 May 2025 15:42To:&nbsp;'Tom Goodhead' &lt;tgoodhead@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Christopher Neill &lt;cneill@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Avril Graham &lt;agraham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Interim Payments&nbsp;Hi Tom/Chris,&nbsp;Good to speak to you the other week about this matter.&nbsp;Following our call, I have held in-principle discussions with Litica about what arrangements may be made to free up some interim payments in the event these are received by PG.&nbsp;Given that the pre-trial settlement window on NOx is still open, our discussions focused on amendments to the priority deeds should there be a liability win on BHP.&nbsp;On the basis that the liability decision was final&nbsp;in PG’s favour (and subject to co-insurers thoughts, what the judgment says, other circumstances at the time etc), Litica would be open to:Amending the BHP PD to the below structure (per my email of 17 April)Should PG wish to reduce the LOI of the policy for the quantum stage of the proceedings, accepting around 60-70p in the pound for any early D&amp;C payment. The early D&amp;C payment would be paid in relation to any amount of the LOI that you wished to take “off-risk”. This would: i.) reduce the amount of LOI held in escrow at stage 1 of the waterfall, and ii.) paid at stage 2, consequently freeing up additional funds for PG’s opex.The 60-70p figure is subject to increase with time to reflect the time value for money of such a deal, and the extent to which insurers would remain on risk before the restructuring is finalised.We would imagine that other co-insurers would also be agreeable to this haircut figure, with each insurer bearing the same % reduction of their LOI.&nbsp;I understand that you intend to instruct costs counsel to advise the extent to which the LOI could be reduced for the stage 2 quantum trial, but in the interim could you let us know your thoughts on the above?&nbsp;Finally, the above proposal would amend the existing documentation under the wrapper, however a number of documents from August 2024 relating to the original structure of the deal are still outstanding. Litica have pointed out that, for obvious reasons, these documents must be completed before any further discussions can feasibly continue on this new structure.&nbsp;I will send over the documents and status of these outstanding documents in a separate email. Please can these be completed?&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)FINEX Global&nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116)On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.comwww.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW.Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At WTW we work flexibly - whilst you may receive emails from me out of working hours, I do not expect a response or action outside your own working hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;From:&nbsp;Tom Goodhead &lt;tgoodhead@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;24 April 2025 10:25To:&nbsp;James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Avril Graham &lt;agraham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Christopher Neill &lt;cneill@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: Interim Payments&nbsp;To give a bit of a preview to the call and to allow some time to think before it, I have had some discussions with Gramercy following a meeting on Monday.&nbsp;As you may be aware (or we should make the insurers aware), there are various settlement discussions now advancing on NOx: 1. Nissan 2.Renault 3. Mercedes 4. Suzuki 5. FordIn relation to BHP, we are hopeful of receiving judgment before the summer break starts (i.e. end of July) and in the(hopefully) very likely event we are successful, we will be seeking an interim payment on account of costs in a sum that will be 60% of 250m pounds+. Realistically, I expect an interim between 60-100m pounds.PG has agreed terms for additional financing from Gramercy of circa $100m ($75m on BHP and $25m on NOx) to provide us with runway through to end of Q1 2027In the event of two or more of the NOx cases and/or one of NOx and BHP positive liability judgment coming in, Gramercy are hoping and PG is looking to refinance Gramercy out (either all four facilities or the BHP ones) at a cheaper rate.&nbsp;I am cognisant of the fact that CPI policies, associated with our Gramercy facilities, are portable in a refi (with the consent of the insurers) and that there is an overlap of insurers between the CPI/ATE layers.&nbsp;The “issue” therefore that arises in terms of PG seeking to free up the interim payment on BHP, to use for opex, rather than hold on trust against any future adverse costs payments may be resolved by virtue of a simultaneous refinance of PG’s entire debt stack (or the BHP part) of the Brazilian lenders and Gramercy, transferring the CPI policies to a new (partially at least) insurance wrapped facility, either prepaying the D&amp;C premia or restructuring the policies and/or purchasing JPI against the judgment being overturned on appeal.&nbsp;You will no doubt have some more sophisticated thoughts than mine on this but I wanted to set this out for the purposes of discussion and also to see how Willis might be able to support a refi process.&nbsp;ThanksTom&nbsp;Tom&nbsp;Goodhead​​​​Chief&nbsp;Executive&nbsp;Officer&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Global&nbsp;Managing&nbsp;PartnerThis email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and "}

