Subject:&nbsp;Quantum Sample Claimant: Discontinuance
&nbsp;


Hi Anna, Helena,




We write in relation to&nbsp;an issue that has arisen in relation to Sample Claimants on the NOx Quantum workstreams. As background, the parties have agreed 40 Sample Claimants across the 5 Lead OEMs (8 each) to participate in the Quantum Trial in October 2026.
 These Sample Claimants have been selected in equal number by the Claimants and Defendants, and once selected, have to proceed as if they were an individual Claimant and complete the usual steps of litigation e.g. providing individual pleadings, witness evidence
 etc.&nbsp;




One such Claimant who was nominated to be a Sample Claimant, Marie O Brien (a Renault PG client) was agreed by the parties as a Sample Claimant on 15 August 2025. Since her nomination, however, she has&nbsp;refused to provide us with updated instructions in respect
 of her claim despite multiple chasers and warnings. As such, we&nbsp;were left with no option but to propose to the Lead Defendants on 19 September that she be removed from the list of Sample Claimants.&nbsp;




The Defendants have agreed for her to be removed subject to: (i) a replacement Sample Claimant being nominated in her stead; and (ii) PG discontinuing her claims on the basis of her lack of engagement. While we do not necessarily agree with (ii), it is hard
 to justify pursuing the claim of a Claimant who just refuses to engage with the litigation.&nbsp;The Managing Judges have also previously made comments at Progress Meetings that Claimants who sign up for Litigation must be expected to participate as if it were
 normal litigation.&nbsp;




We are hoping to get a steer from the Judge at tomorrow's PM if he agrees with the Ds. If he does, then we will have to work out the mechanics of discontinuance. In any event, and for future instances where Claimants become disengaged with the Sample Claimant
 process (a realistic prospect from experience on VW1), we must establish the procedure for how we deal with such Claimants.&nbsp;




Per the Renault GLO, a Sample C needs the permission of the Court to discontinue their claim. In the general course of litigation, in circumstances where a firm cannot get instructions from an individual, they would (after providing them with sufficient warning),
 apply to come off the Court record. This however would not be a cure to the issue in this instance as it would simply leave the client &quot;in the wild&quot;, liable to be pursued by the Defendant for their costs, and the Sample Claimant process would be slowed sufficiently
 that it does not seem likely to be in the interests of the wider cohort to take this course of action.




The other option is to seek to invite the Claimant Committee to use the power of delegated authority, conferred on them by the Litigation Group Agreement / Litigation Management Agreement and the CFA, to provide their instructions to discontinue this claim
 (and future claims). We understand that this LGA / LMA was being reviewed by Mark Friston following the CMH judgment last year.

@Sarah&nbsp;- can you advise us on the status of the LGA? Similarly, if you have come across this issue before your thoughts would be welcome.&nbsp;




Pending us having the right to take this course of action, Our proposed approach is to:&nbsp;


Write to the client one last time (via email and registered post) warning her that if she refuses to engage with us, we will have no choice but to take instructions from the Claimant Committee (CC)
 to discontinue her claim; and&nbsp;

Write to Renault CC and get their consent to make the requisite application.&nbsp;





We discussed this point with Martyn earlier today as part of the PM prep, and he&nbsp;(it seemed from experience) was very wary about being able to use a CC to discontinue a Claimants claim and he&nbsp;seems to be of the view that we cannot discontinue a client's claim
 without their express instructions. &nbsp;The current wording in the Renault retainers (&quot;In appointing the Committee as agents in respect of the conduct of the litigation, the parties to this Agreement irrevocably grant the Committee the power to: [...].
 b. Discontinue any and all claims&quot;) however&nbsp;seems to suggest that the CC do have that power.




Despite this being flagged to Martyn, he is worried that this clause is too wide and we must be circumspect in invoking it. He has asked us to check with Ben Williams on this point.&nbsp;




We are keen to establish the position of the LGA and seek additional comfort advice from counsel on being able to take instructions from the CC given the warning from Martyn.&nbsp;




Grateful for your thoughts.&nbsp;

































