Hi James, all -&nbsp;

In advance of our call and as foreshadowed by our request below, it would be good if the PG team could give detailed thought to the following and be ready to give evidence-based
 answers (if not on the call itself, shortly afterwards).&nbsp;

In circumstances where the Court has previously granted Lead Claimant firm status, it ought to be straightforward to track these points. We have also, collectively,
 touched on almost all of these in previous calls, but it would be helpful to have answers and forward-looking information in one place.&nbsp;

At a high level, the court must / must have been satisfied that the firm has/had the means and experience necessary to coordinate and represent a large group effectively
 (see eg AB v John Wyeth &amp; Brother Ltd [1993] 4 Med LR 289; AB v Leeds Teaching Hospitals NHS Trust [2005] 1 WLR 2206 (CA); The Atomic Veterans Litigation [2010] EWHC 1626 (QB); Various Claimants v GSK (Seroxat Litigation) [2013] EWHC 1180 (QB); Municipio&nbsp;de
 Mariana v BHP Group [2020] EWHC 2930 (TCC)).

In particular:&nbsp;



Experience and expertise: proven history managing large-scale or complex group litigation.


Organisational capacity: sufficient staff, infrastructure, and systems for communication and disclosure (and now, trial management).


Financial resources: adequate funding or arrangements to sustain the litigation through to conclusion.


Effective communication systems: ability to liaise efficiently with all claimants and obtain instructions.


Leadership and coordination: capacity to act as the central point of contact and work cooperatively with others.


Legal team quality: qualified, stable team with relevant seniority and defined roles.


Case management: coherent, realistic strategy for procedural management and compliance with court directions.

Integrity and independence: absence of conflicts of interest and fair representation of all claimants.






This is a non-exhaustive list, but as above hopefully self-explanatory and helpful.&nbsp;




Look forward to speaking.&nbsp;




Kind regards&nbsp;
Matt&nbsp;










Matt Evans&nbsp;




