2024, and 14 August 2025 (“Credit Agreements”).&nbsp;Structure Overview&nbsp;Gramercy Guarantee&nbsp;We confirmed that obtaining a guarantee from Gramercy would be challenging, and Toremis appeared comfortable proceeding without it, provided that the structure outlined below is acceptable.&nbsp;Cross-CollateralisationThis is a potential dealbreaker for at least some of the cases requiring top-ups. Toremis is seeking a mechanism akin to the existing overarching deed (“Wrapper”) but limited to cross-collateralisation among the Nox cases only.&nbsp;Because the risk profile varies across the Nox cases, they have proposed a separate cross-collateralisation agreement that would apply to the eight top-up policies. Toremis is aware of the existing cross-collateralisation provisions within the Wrapper and is comfortable that the new arrangement would rank below these, as altering the current priority would require changes to the existing structure.&nbsp;We will need to discuss further how the cross-collateralisation of the additional layers would coexist within the waterfall, particularly in relation to the cross-collateralisation of emissions proceeds in the 4th layer of the BHP ATE policy (as formalised in the Reimbursement Deed), which sets out payment of reimbursement out of 50% of PG net proceeds from Nox cases after taxes, insurers, counsel, and payments to funders, including Gramercy facilities.&nbsp;Economic Terms of the Top-Up&nbsp;The upfront premium, D&amp;C, reimbursement of adverse costs, and other terms and conditions would mirror those of the existing layers of the ATE policies and the individual Priorities Deeds. This additional layer would sit above the current layers with respect to reimbursements and D&amp;C premiums payable within each Nox case.&nbsp;As discussed, to cover insurers’ risk arising from the AAE to be given to defendants, they would require PG to provide an indemnity for any potential claims paid by the insurers that fall within the exclusion