&nbsp;


Dear Board,
&nbsp;
We write further to the emails exchanged between Michael Burke, Sarah Bingham and Anna Varga dated 15 and 16 October
 2025 as they relate to the Litigation Management Agreement (&quot;LMA&quot;)&nbsp;and Agency Agreement (&quot;AA&quot;). This email is intended to be a protected disclosure.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
As can be seen from the First Witness Statement of Alicia Alinia dated 30 September at paragraph 35.1 - she has confirmed
 (and verified with a statement of truth): &quot;For the avoidance of doubt, Gramercy has no control over the day-to-day management or decisions of the Firm or the Partner, and nor does Gramercy have any role in the conduct, control or management of the NOx Group
 Litigations.&quot;
&nbsp;
Further, at 35.3, it is confirmed that &quot;Its board of directors is entirely independent of Gramercy, and nothing in
 its financial arrangements inhibits the Firm from actively, and independently, pursuing the Proceedings in the best interests of its clients.&quot;
&nbsp;
On account of the email sent by Anna Varga on 16 October at 8.57AM in which she explicitly instructs Sarah Bingham to
 instruct Stewarts (who are retained by Gramercy - see email from Joshua Katz dated 13.05.2025 confirming this) to provide input into documents central to the NOx Litigation - the statement at 35.1 is now categorically untrue. Senior management has instructed
 a member of staff to engage with a firm which is instructed by Gramercy to review both Pan NOx and PG specific documents. This position therefore must be corrected to the Court and to the Defendants (and to the PG staff) where the firm have represented that
 Gramercy does not have input into the conduct of the&nbsp;Litigations (in&nbsp;the case of to the Court - with a signed statement of truth).&nbsp;
&nbsp;
With regard to 35.3 - see the email from Julian Chamberlayne on 9 September 2025 at 11.30 in which he confirms that
 &quot;I have now heard from Gramercy that, based on our review, they are content with you proceeding with the revised agency agreements&quot;&nbsp;. In circumstances where Stewarts, instructed by Gramercy, are reviewing and providing approval in respect of key NOx
 documents and requiring PG to await execution prior to their approval, it cannot be true to state that the Firm can independently pursue the Proceedings (and is in fact doing so) cannot be true.&nbsp;

We completely understand that a litigation funder&nbsp;will have a legitimate interest in reviewing certain documents, and
 may quite properly wish to have input where those documents either (a) give rise to a financial liability on the funder, or (b) relate to compliance with the funding terms.


We also acknowledge PG’s position under the funding arrangements in particular, the covenant requiring PG to obtain
 the funder’s prior approval before engaging any new law firm or barrister to whom PG grants a contingent interest. This requirement exists because such engagements could create a financial exposure that Gramercy may ultimately need to meet.


However, the Pan-NOx Agency Agreement&nbsp;does not&nbsp;fall within that category. It does not create any contingent interests,
 nor does it involve any cost contribution obligations from the Non-Lead Firms. Its purpose is purely to define the principal–agent relationship&nbsp;between Pogust Goodhead (as Lead Solicitor) and the Non-Lead Firms, governing the conduct, management, strategy
 decisions and coordination of the litigation. In short, it enables PG to act independently as Lead Solicitor within the proceedings.


Similarly, the Litigation Management Agreement is not a funding or liability document. Rather, it is an agreement between
 the Client Committee and Pogust Goodhead, setting out the instructions, authority and governance framework&nbsp;under which PG acts for the claimants within each GLO. It defines procedural and operational responsibilities, not financial exposure, and is an essential
 component of PG’s authority to manage the litigation.


The level of control that Gramercy has sought to exercise over these documents &nbsp;which govern solicitor-client relationships,
 not funder obligations&nbsp; goes far beyond the usual parameters of a lender–borrower relationship. It represents an overreach into the conduct and management of the litigation itself, rather than oversight of financial risk. That distinction is fundamental, and
 Gramercy’s continued involvement in these matters strays beyond what is permissible for a funder maintaining a proper arm’s-length relationship with the litigation.

It therefore&nbsp;appears that our collective position is that PG is&nbsp;now openly operating in a world where the funder of
 the firm has direct management/control of aspects of the litigation - and that control is on-going, despite representations&nbsp;to the contrary made to the Court, the Defendants and to the staff. This creates significant issues for the staff working on these Litigations
 both in our duties to our clients and to the clients of the other firms in our firm's role as Lead Solicitors.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
Can you please advise on the following:
&nbsp;



why representations have been made to the Court, Defendants and staff that Gramercy do not have any role in the conduct, control or management of the NOx Group Litigations when
 plainly - they do and continue to do so;




whether the Board intends to update the Court as to the true position; and




whether the Board intends to inform the clients and other claimant firms of Gramercy's input into documents that have a direct bearing on costs and client instructions.

&nbsp;
We are continuing to consider this on-going issue as more information comes to light. All of our rights are reserved.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
Kind regards,
&nbsp;
Michael, Priyanka, Sarah

&nbsp;

































