Dear Anna,&nbsp;


Thank you for your email.&nbsp;


We have some overarching points to raise before addressing the points in your email regarding the Side Letter Agency Agreement
 (“SL AA”).&nbsp;


First and foremost, we have raised serious and significant issues regarding how traumatic we, and the entire team, are finding
 this process and that we have genuine concerns for the mental health and well-being of our staff. We further flagged that the proposal creates a two-tier structure amongst the wider team, which risks alienating individual members of the staff. Your email worryingly
 does not address any of those concerns, nor does it even mention them.&nbsp;


We also note that various junior staff within the NOx department have been enlisted to send out correspondence, including on weekends,
 relating to non-common work and non-PDD Trial matters (see, for example, an email from Hannah Varghese at 15.32 on Saturday, 4 October 2025). Requiring the most junior staff to become actively involved in non-common work issues, while on the other hand attempting
 to assert that “everything we have done or are intending to do is with the best interests of our clients as well as our staff in mind first and foremost” is simply unreal. Correspondence of that nature and related to non-common work should be sent by
 an individual not working on critical workstreams.&nbsp;&nbsp;


Each of the case leads are committed to finding a co-operative solution to the issues that are being raised. That is why we are
 proactively engaging in discussions and, in aid of assisting the firm, why we have continued to raise points so as to avoid the firm falling foul of issues that they may otherwise be presented with, e.g. noting potential additional disclosure obligations re.
 documents referred to in witness statements. You also have said that you “welcome any specific input we wish to contribute”. This is difficult to contemplate as being a genuine offer in circumstances where a) the SL AA was provided by Leigh Day (“LD”),
 not you nor the Board, despite Pogust Goodhead (“PG”) being our employer; and b) where we have previously sought to provide input (e.g. meeting with Alicia Alinia at 18:00 Monday 29 September 2025) and you have not engaged with it. Instead, you have
 sent us an email that dismisses our concerns and informs us that our description of the SL AA is incorrect, while at the same time stating that your intention is for this email to now bring these discussions to a conclusion. This is neither helpful nor is
 it in the spirit of co-operation.&nbsp;&nbsp;


For the avoidance of any doubt, we do not consent to the SL AA in its current form. No representations can be made to the Court
 at the PTR on 6 October that this has been put in place and is agreed. To the extent that you and the Board believe you can bind individuals named in the SL AA (or annexes) by virtue of it being a ‘firm decision’ we disagree for reasons set out below. Every
 individual subject to the agreement either does not consent to it (the case leads) or, as far as we are aware, has never seen any version of it and so is unable to have provided consent.&nbsp; We accept of course, that Counsel can inform the Court (on 6 October
 or otherwise) that it is being drafted and the intention is that it will be put in place in due course.&nbsp;&nbsp;


If we, as the collective case leads, are to work collaboratively with the Board and you, and HR, to establish how we can move forward
 amicably, then it requires serious engagement from you and the Board, and not a dismissal of our concerns.&nbsp;&nbsp;


We trust that this email will allow for a more collaborative approach going forward.&nbsp;


On the points in your email:&nbsp;


1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Purpose and Effect of the Side Letter&nbsp;

You explained that PG’s proposed role is intended to operate “exactly as envisaged”
within the existing Pan-NOx AA framework, and that the SL AA will simply implement a delegation of common work from LD to PG personnel. If that were indeed the case, a separate SL AA would not be necessary; indeed, this accords with our own understanding
 that the new arrangement is entirely novel.&nbsp;&nbsp;


&nbsp;


You say this agreement is to “ensure that [we] can continue to work as currently on the existing work plans.” Having
 reviewed the SL AA, it is clear that it goes further than the delegation provisions in the Pan-NOx AA, and that is precisely why it requires a standalone agreement. The SL AA imposes materially different terms in at least two key respects:&nbsp;


&nbsp;


a.
Formal separation of supervision and control&nbsp;


The SL AA introduces an unusual structure whereby PG personnel will perform common work solely under the direction of LD, but remain
 employed and supervised by PG. Indeed, we understand that the Board intends for PG personnel to carry out generic/firm-specific work under PG’s direction. This formalises a split between line management and professional control that is not addressed, let alone
 authorised, in the Pan-NOx AA. That agreement permits delegation of work to another firm, not delegation of the
supervision of one firm’s employees to another while the employer retains all employment obligations.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


b.
Governance over PG’s internal personnel decisions&nbsp;


The SL AA (via Annex B) requires LD’s consultation and agreement before PG can add or remove PG personnel from common work. That
 means PG cannot unilaterally deploy or reassign its own staff in this litigation without LD’s approval. This imposes external governance over PG’s workforce, a control that is not contemplated in the Pan-NOx AA and which cuts across PG’s autonomy as an employer.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


The SL AA changes the status quo in material ways such that it cannot be said that what is being proposed is
 “exactly what we have all been working under” or that “this will not practically change much day to day.”&nbsp;&nbsp;


&nbsp;


2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Tension with Employment Contracts&nbsp;

Because the SL AA goes beyond what is authorised in the Pan-NOx AA, it creates direct tension with the terms of PG employment contracts.
 You advise that “there will be no change to your employment contract” and that “consent by employees is not necessary for PG to execute the agency agreement.” We respectfully disagree.&nbsp;


Clause 2(b) of the SL AA states that “PG Personnel shall perform such Common Work … solely under the direction of, and reporting
 solely to, Leigh Day.” This directly conflicts with multiple provisions that we presume appear in each individual employee contract:&nbsp;


&nbsp;


a.
Duty of obedience and supervision&nbsp;


Under Clause 4/Duties of the employment contract (which we presume is a standard term of contract for all PG employees), PG employees
 are contractually required to comply with PG’s directions and report to PG line managers. They are to “devote the whole of their time, attention and abilities to the business of the Company.” The SL AA seeks to override that by imposing a structure
 in which we report solely to LD in respect of a major portion of our work, without any formal secondment or variation of contract.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


b.
Regulatory oversight&nbsp;


The split between day-to-day supervision (by LD) and formal employment responsibility (by PG) creates regulatory ambiguity under
 the SRA Code of Conduct. Who is the supervising solicitor? Are there two supervising solicitors, one from Leigh Day and another from PG? Which firm bears responsibility for ensuring compliance with professional standards?&nbsp;&nbsp;


&nbsp;


These are not academic questions - they carry regulatory and indemnity implications.&nbsp;&nbsp;


&nbsp;


c. Consent and legal clarity&nbsp;


The arrangement introduces a dual-control model, but without staff consent, proper transparency, or any framework setting out lines
 of accountability. As PG remains the employer, yet LD directs our work in practice, the legal and ethical basis for this arrangement is unclear, particularly when conflicts, confidentiality obligations, or errors arise.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Professional Indemnity Insurance Risk&nbsp;
The SL AA structure also raises serious questions around insurance cover. If PG personnel are performing common work solely under
 LD’s direction (as the SL AA confirms), then it is not clear whose professional indemnity (“PI”) insurance would respond in the event of a professional error. This is in circumstances where PG remains the/our employer and is responsible for holding
 PI cover for its staff. But LD&nbsp;is envisaged as exercising the day to-day control and making the litigation decisions. The SRA expects supervision and insurance liability to be aligned. That is not the case here.&nbsp;

&nbsp;


This is not hypothetical. A clear example of this risk occurred this week when LD made a costs offer to the Mercedes Defendants
 without PG’s knowledge or approval from PG's insurer - despite the fact that PG personnel had prepared the underlying work. If that offer proves problematic, it is unclear whether PG or LD (or both) would be liable and, more importantly,
whose insurer would be on risk. That ambiguity creates risk for both firms and undermines the clarity of the delegation framework set out in the Pan-NOx AA.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


This is precisely why the Pan-NOx AA structured delegation at the firm level: to avoid splitting supervision and risk in
 a way that confuses accountability. The SL AA unpicks that structure and introduces unnecessary exposure.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


We refer you to 17(a) of the PTR skeleton argument filed by Mercedes where the Defendants have drawn the Court’s attention to this
 issue.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Client Consent&nbsp;

If PG wishes for us and other PG staff to be instructed by, and report solely to, a firm other than PG, then this requires our
 express consent, and (we believe) the informed consent of the clients whom we act on behalf of (which will not only be just the clients of PG, but&nbsp; may extent to all clients within every GLO if the work is to be Pan-NOx). If PG personnel are to carry out work
 on those clients’ cases under the direction of a different firm, the clients must be told who is supervising that work and must provide informed instructions to that effect. We refer you to Rule 1.4 of the Code of Conduct, which requires solicitors to ensure
 clients are in a position to make informed decisions about their representation. You cannot unilaterally impose this on us, or on any of the PG staff, nor PG’s clients as your email&nbsp; suggests you can.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
You cannot unilaterally impose a change of supervisory firm on your employees or on the clients, as your email equally suggests. The assumption that this arrangement can simply be implemented without individual or client-level engagement is not supported by
 the regulatory framework. This concern is not novel: post our comments in this regard, this is also expressly identified in the skeleton argument filed by Mercedes at paragraph 17(b), where they raise the potential for regulatory impropriety in precisely these
 circumstances.&nbsp;


Turning to other points specifically raised in your email:&nbsp;&nbsp;


·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
You note that we are obliged to work our notice periods. Thank you for pointing this out again, although we note it is not consistent with representations
 that PG has made to the Defendants and Court that the timing of our last days are subject to ongoing discussion. You go on to say that once our notice period has expired, we are free to commence working for JLG, should we so choose. Collectively, none of us
 have informed you of our plans post-PG.&nbsp;&nbsp;

&nbsp;


·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
You have advised that LD and counsel have approved this solution. Please provide us with this correspondence (including the advice the firm has
 taken from counsel – as originally requested). We note that as per Martyn Day’s email on 3 October at 13.56 that “Alicia has asked that we leave these issues for PG to sort out with the team. We await the outcome of those discussions and so no need for
 the meeting this afternoon.” In circumstances where LD are waiting for PG to “sort out these issues with the team” and that they will “await the outcome”, we fail to understand how LD have approved the solution.&nbsp;

&nbsp;


·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
You advise that you are “now interviewing so with time this work-stream will be handed over”. Please explain which work-stream? Does this
 relate to non-common work?&nbsp; 

We repeat that we are keen to try and work together to find a collaborative solution to these issues, not least because of the
 importance of the upcoming PDD Trial beginning in a little over a week’s time and for which we are all deeply invested in and, in truth, where all of this is causing a huge and detrimental distraction. However, it is plain to us that the SL AA is plainly more
 than a simple delegation mechanism. It alters the control and supervision structure, restricts PG’s governance over its own staff, creates clear tension with employment contracts and regulatory obligations, and places work done on behalf of the PG&nbsp;clients
 under the instruction of an external law firm (without any proposal for client consent to be obtained). If there is to be a material change, it needs to be handled transparently and within a proper legal framework. We raise these points constructively and
 in good faith, with the shared goal of ensuring compliance and continuing to act in our clients’ best interests.&nbsp;


We trust these concerns now demonstrate that the issues raised in the draft SL AA document need to be engaged with, and we look
 forward to hearing from you on those issues and how they can be addressed, such that we can move forward, together.&nbsp;&nbsp; The Case Leads&nbsp;


Michael Burke, Priyanka Gladson, Matthew Hunt, Sarah Bingham &amp; Georgina Fallows&nbsp;


