odhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matt Hunt &lt;mhunt@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matthew Newbould &lt;mnewbould@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Dilan Alma &lt;dalma@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation - Toremis&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Thanks for your email Helena.&nbsp;I have spoken to Toremis this morning to flesh out how they foresee the cross-collateralisation point working and confirm a few of your queries.They are happy for client proceeds to be ring-fenced. Very much like the other cross-collateralisation documents you have in place they foresee the cross-collateralisation attaching to PG’s proceeds of the claims (i.e. those monies PG receives once the proceeds have moved through the waterfall of each PD). Whilst there would be an individual excess policy attaching to each matter, the cross-collateralisation document would work so it is as if there is one overarching policy sitting over all of these top-ups, so Toremis could apply any recoveries against a loss on one particular policy. For example, if there was a £2m claim on the Ford top-up but all the other matters recovered proceeds, these proceeds would be applied to that £2m loss to make Toremis whole. We see this structure being advantageous to PG as: i.) overall the cost of insurance will be reduced and ii.) all matters will be covered – Toremis would not cherry-pick the strongest matters and only offer insurance against these. Separately Toremis would need to be added to the individual PDs in this matter which is the usual process when an excess layer is added. They would slot into the equivalent position of a 2nd/3rd/4th layer insurer (as applicable).Toremis are aware of the pre-existing cross-collateralisation provisions (Nathan Hull was head of ATE at VALE when these documents were put in place). They are comfortable with the fact that they would sit below these (as to do otherwise would need to unwind the provisions that are in place).@Anna Varga @Matt Hunt,@Matthew Newbould Re the merits call later – Toremis have no particular queries the case leads would need to be aware of. It will essentially be fleshing out the bullet-point queries sent to Counsel (below) / giving a “state of the nation” walk-through of the merits of the matter, similar to the call conducted with the existing insurers ahead of the PDD trial.&nbsp;Confirming, at a high level, that, with the benefit of having now reviewed the expert and factual evidence (and the detailed legal position taken by the defendants) the merits remain at least as good as the original assessments (including confirming what the relevant percentage assessment is for each of the defendants).Confirming the position on the merits in relation to: (1) whether each of the defendants' vehicles contained unlawful defeat devices; (2) whether there are any viable article 5 defences available to the defendants; and (3) to the extent separate to (1) and (2), the position on the existence of a breach of statutory duty and/or a breach of contract.&nbsp;We also spoke to them about this morning’s piece in the Times. They were not too concerned about it as they did not view it as affecting the underlying merits of the case. We do not think it will come up on today’s call.&nbsp;I hope that assists. I will come back to you with your queries re Mosaic separately as I wanted to get these points to you and the team soonest ahead of the Toremis call.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX Global &nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116) On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.com www.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW. Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At WTW we work flexibly - whilst you may receive emails from me out of working hours, I do not expect a response or action outside your own working hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;From: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 04 November 2025 16:51To: James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Dilan Alma &lt;dalma@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation&nbsp;Hi James, &nbsp;Thank you for your email. This is encouraging news, and we appreciate your efforts in moving this forward.&nbsp;With regards to the additional points, I have attached a further advice on Mercedes which may be helpful. We can also arrange for Anna and a couple of the case leads to meet with Toremis tomorrow to discuss further. The team have availability between 13.30 and 17.00. &nbsp;Please let me know whether Toremis are able to accommodate.&nbsp;With regards to the cross-collateralisation point, we do have a few questions which we are hoping you can address:&nbsp;Client proceeds – please could you clarify whether cross-collateralisation has any impact on client damages? Specifically, can you confirm that it does not affect client recoveries beyond the insurer’s entitlements?Scope - how exactly will cross-collateralisation operate i.e., what types of insurer recoveries (e.g., paid claims, contingent premiums) are in scope?Existing arrangements - would there be any impact on the wrapper arrangements already in place? Can we keep them completely separate?We would also like to explore the Mosaic option you mentioned yesterday in tandem. Please could you provide some additional detail on the following points: 1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Structure - the policy would need to safeguard client interests, could the policy expressly designate claimants as third-party beneficiaries? If not, would it be possible for the insurer to issue a side letter, deed of benefit, or assignment giving clients direct enforceable rights? &nbsp;2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Claims - it would be helpful if you could outline the claims process, including how coverage applies when the insured is not the named party to the adverse costs order.3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Protections for PG – we would need to ensure that the firm is not liable if the insurer declines cover for reasons unrelated to misconduct. How can PG navigate this risk?4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Claim payments - this structure could be scrutinised by the defendants and therefore it would assist if any claims monies could be directly paid to the defendant or PGs client account.&nbsp;Many thanks Helena &nbsp;​​​​Helena&nbsp;YearwoodRisk&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Compliance&nbsp;DirectorThis email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp