2th December. &nbsp;&nbsp;We highlight that Toremis requires Gramercy to sign an acknowledgment of the deed as per Schedule 5 of the draft Overarching Priority Deed to confirm cover is bound.&nbsp;The initial limit of indemnity set out above was based on 60% incurred costs and 100% approved costs to 31 July. The judge then ordered that claimant firms need to secure an LOI of 64% incurred costs and 100% approved costs to 31 July. This increases the overall LOI top-up required by £1.8m. The Claimants will be liable for the Defendant's costs in relation to the funding disclosure applications. The Defendants are claiming £500k, which will be covered by the existing ATE policies. This LOI will also need to be replenished in the top-up, bringing the required top-up to £2.3m. We are pushing Toremis to provide the terms for the additional amounts asap. &nbsp;&nbsp;Following the funding disclosure application hearing on 21 November, Constable J ordered that the adequate level of ATE is 64% of incurred costs. As a result of this ruling, he revised the timetable for the defendants to issue any applications for security for costs and ordered that the claimants have until 12th December to confirm to the defendants their level of coverage. If 64% is not achieved at such time, the defendants will issue a security for costs application by 19th December. If such applications are made for the sole reason that PG has not obtained 64%, PG will lose those applications, and the cost of that will far outweigh the cost of the expected premiums (not to mention wasting valuable human resources required for the running of the case). By way of comparison, 13 applications were made against PG to disclose the funding documents. The defendants' aggregate statement of costs comes to over £500k. The cost of any security for costs application will be sub"}

