Subject:&nbsp;Formal grievance
&nbsp;


Dear Raminder,




Please consider this a formal grievance in accordance with the firm's grievance policy (v1.0, 4 July 2024).




Acas early reconciliation process




This is a unique grievance for two reasons.&nbsp; First, the grievance is with senior leadership of the firm, whom I understand will have responsibility for considering whether to uphold or dismiss it.&nbsp; Second, its subject matter has been repeatedly ignored or dismissed
 by that leadership.&nbsp; I therefore do not believe that it will be dealt with properly, and as set out below believe the firm has no incentive to deal with it properly.




The formal grievance process is therefore, in my view, not entirely adequate.&nbsp; Accordingly, whilst I ask that you please treat this message as raising a formal grievance, please also consider it notification that I intend to contact Acas to engage the early
 reconciliation process.




Adverse health effects




The stress that I feel, and the mental health effects of PG's various reactions to the case leads' resignation, are documented in my resignation letter and various emails to the board (see, e.g., 3 October email from Michael Burke at 12:29, “we cannot stress
 enough how traumatic we and the entire team are finding this process, and that it is happening on the eve of trial”).&nbsp;&nbsp;

&nbsp;

I have not received even a bare acknowledgement of my stress, let alone any sympathy or attempt to address my concerns.&nbsp; Requests for explanations and information are consistently ignored (see, e.g., 9 October email from Michael Burke at 18:14).&nbsp; Attempting
 to run the PDD Trial, whilst being repeatedly distracted by the firm's behaviour, is affecting my health.&nbsp; A recent example is that, on 24 October 2025, the emissions &quot;case leads&quot; had a meeting at which quantum resourcing was discussed.&nbsp; In response to the
 suggestion that Peter Gallagher be involved, rather than a reasoned explanation be given, Anna Varga shouted that &quot;I
[Anna]&nbsp;know you are all going to JLG.&quot;&nbsp; The suggestion that I am motivated by some sort of conspiracy, rather than my clients' best interests, is insulting and has no basis in reality.




Having reflected, I have spoken to a mental health nurse, using my health insurance provided by the firm, for advice on how to address this stress.&nbsp; The nurse advised me immediately to self-certify as off work sick with stress, and seek an appointment with
 a doctor to see if more than a week off is appropriate.

&nbsp;

In any other circumstances I would do that, but at the moment I am torn as I care deeply about the trial and our clients.&nbsp; The firm’s combination of silence and aggression leads me to infer that the firm, aware of my devotion to the trial, clients and colleagues,
 is anticipating that I will simply carry on regardless.&nbsp; This inference only worsens my state.&nbsp; Finally, whilst the firm needs me until the PCD section of the PDD Trial is complete on 28 November, from that point I believe that the firm will consider me dead
 weight.




Requested resolution




Please provide a proposal for the following:



An acknowledgement of stress that the firm's treatment of the case leads has caused.


An apology for the same.


Compensation.





The firm must have been aware of the health effects of its treatment of staff for a long time (my resignation letter explained my stress months ago), and so should be able to provide the above promptly.




I look forward to hearing from you.




Kind regards,




Matt






























​​​​































