Hi Sophia,
&nbsp;
The Defendants could raise a variety of arguments including that:
&nbsp;

The claims have not been resolved, so it is premature to decide who is the winner and costs should be reserved.
I think you will win this issue.If they have made Part 36 offers, the usual position is that costs are reserved. Even if no Part 36 offers made, they may
 argue that the same should apply to Calderbank offer. If Part 36 offers have been made, it is likely that the costs will be reserved. If only Calderbank, more complicated but I expect you’d have the better of the argument.Even if a costs order is made, the Defendants would be unable to recover any interim payment from the Claimants, so it should
 not be ordered now and/or should be stayed pending an appeal. 
If the solicitors give an undertaking to repay any costs, then you should be able to overcome this issue. If not, then the costs order could well be stayed.No costs order should be made in respect of DBA claimants (as there can be no interim liability under DBAs). [And for CFAs,
 they may query if the clients’ CFA included a right to be paid on an interim basis].
There is no authority on this and it is not straightforward. I had a case in the CAT recently where an interim payment was not ordered as a result of this argument and it was left to the Costs judge. But this should only affect the share
 attributable to DBA cases, disbursements could still be ordered to be paid (other than counsel’s fees) if provided for in the DBAs, and you have decent arguments that there should be an interim payment for everything.
