Dear Tom,




Please see attached a draft of the letter with my suggested amendments and comments.




The most important point here is to make sure that what is said about PG’s funding agreements is accurate and not misleading by omission, because anything that isn’t quite right (or a relevant funding arrangement not included) will doubtless be seized on down
 the line.




On the ATE assumptions, my views are as follows (and I know these are shared by Simon):





We think that future costs for ADR and Experts should be included in the relevant expenditure calculations. Despite not having been assessed by the court yet, those costs would be incurred within the relevant period and therefore
 will be taken into account when assessing of sufficiency of cover. It would be reasonable at this stage to take the Claimants’ offers against those phases as the starting point for what we say is required, but we must recognise that leaving those phases out
 altogether isn’t going to be defensible before the court. So if para 9a and 9b are to go into the letter (and I can see they may be needed for now if the position on sufficiency of cover is to be maintained) we need to accept internally that there will need
 to be movement on that point (and additional cover) if the position on ATE sufficiency is to be maintained before the court.




Similarly, we don’t think there is a strong case for downgrading the % of incurred costs by 10%. Again, if this is to go into the letter we ought to treat it internally as a holding position while you look to increase cover.





We agree that it is not sensible to disclose in the letter that you are looking to increase cover. Most importantly, that essentially amounts to a concession that what you have is insufficient - and additionally, as you point out, you may not be able to secure
 further cover and we don’t want to box ourselves in.




I hope that helps. I am around tomorrow morning if needed until around 1pm.




Many thanks,




Theo




















